Hi, All,

I have a question about the below data set. 

You can see that dependent variable Y is discrete, while X is continuous.

Now I want to find the relation betwee X and Y like this:

 0<X<b1  Y=1;

b1<X<b2  Y=2;

...

b17<X  Y=18;

What algorithm I shoud use? Does Splus have the related function?

Thank you very much.

To Alll:

S+ 6.1 R1

Win XP SP1

P4 2.4 GHz 1GB RAM

I know I have seen this issue addressed previously but could not find it
in the archives so I apologize for bringing this up again.  The
problem is to calculate the minimum value of all possible
"contiguous" sums in a vector.  Brute force 
code:

nrep <- 10000;

nmonth <- 40;

# Calculate Maximum Cumulative Loss for draws from empirical
distribution

e.max <- matrix(0,nrep,1);

e.sim <- apply( t(ecdf.sim), 1, function (x) {x * discvec); # Apply
discount factors

for (k in 1:nrep) {

e.simk <-  e.sim[k,];  

e.maxpoint <-
min(e.simk);      # Select
the min of the single elements to start

        for (i in
1:(nmonth-1)) {

                for
(j in (i+1):(nmonth)){

                        sm
<- sum(e.simk[i:j])

                        ifelse
( sm < e.maxpoint, sm , e.maxpoint);

                }

        }

        e.max[k]
<- e.maxpoint; 

}

Clearly, I could use a For loop but thought that the problem was
small enough to run from source().  As it stands, this took seven
hours and didn't finish.  Any suggestions on how to vectorize would
be greatly appreciated.

Keith

